1
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:13,600
Good morning, Susan.

2
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:19,500
Good morning, Sam.

3
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:24,200
What's the schedule today?

4
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:24,600
Ten o'clock,

5
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:27,400
telephone FAO Schwarz about the new twin baby dolls.

6
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:27,900
OK.

7
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:31,800
Telephone Mrs. Zaskey at the advertising agency.

8
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:32,540
I did that. Go on.

9
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:34,871
Eleven o'clock, approve the sketches for the toy spaceship.

10
00:00:34,871 --> 00:00:35,400
Where are they?

11
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:37,600
Right here.

12
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:38,885
Did you look at them?

13
00:00:38,885 --> 00:00:40,782
Yes, I did.

14
00:00:40,782 --> 00:00:42,854
What do you think of the spaceship?

15
00:00:42,854 --> 00:00:44,900
I think the kids'll love it.

16
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:50,200
Would you show me the drawings, please?

17
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:55,443
Huh.

18
00:00:55,443 --> 00:00:58,100
Now, what else is on the schedule today?

19
00:00:58,100 --> 00:00:59,800
Well, at one o'clock

20
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:01,400
you have a lunch appointment with Mr. Levine,

21
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,148
the client from the Toytown Stores.

22
00:01:03,148 --> 00:01:04,500
Where?

23
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:05,701
At Rossano's.

24
00:01:05,701 --> 00:01:08,300
Hmm. Anything else?

25
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:09,000
At four o'clock,

26
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:09,900
you have a meeting with the production staff

27
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:11,500
in the conference room.

28
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:13,500
Make sure everybody is at that meeting.

29
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:14,800
Will do.

30
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,532
At six you're meeting, Mr. Ozawa.

31
00:01:17,532 --> 00:01:20,000
Oh, yes. Are his models here?

32
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:20,200
They're in my office.

33
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:21,176
I'd like to see them.

34
00:01:21,176 --> 00:01:22,800
Right.

35
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:28,200
What else?

36
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:30,700
Come on, Sam ...

37
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:33,000
You work too hard, Susan.

38
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,600
When was your last day off?

39
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:37,800
Hmm. I can't remember.

40
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:40,300
You really ought to take some time off.

41
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:41,000
What for?

42
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,400
To enjoy the simple things in life ...

43
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:46,800
I know, Sam.

44
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:48,131
Maybe soon.

45
00:01:48,131 --> 00:01:50,600
To smell the flowers.

46
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:53,700
Oh, wait a minute. What's today's date?

47
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:56,160
Today is the twelfth. Why?

48
00:01:56,160 --> 00:02:03,100
It seems to me I scheduled something else.

49
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:05,200
There's nothing else in the appointment book.

50
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:07,100
I'm sure I did.

51
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:11,100
Oh, well, I'll probably remember it later.

52
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:12,700
I hope it isn't important.

53
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:14,400
Hmm. It's probably nothing.

54
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:15,900
OK, let's get started.

55
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:19,200
Would you call Priscilla Smith at FAO Schwarz, please?

56
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:26,200
Right.

57
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:28,900
These are the models from the Japanese film maker.

58
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:32,800
Thank you. Just put them on my desk.

59
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:37,600
And the new drawings for the toy spaceship.

60
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:46,397
Wonderful.

61
00:02:46,397 --> 00:02:47,900
That was fast.

62
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:48,700
We have a new artist.

63
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:50,300
She's very talented.

64
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:51,400
What time is it, anyway?

65
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:51,800
My watch stopped.

66
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:53,900
It's eleven thirty.

67
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:57,100
What time is my lunch date with Bill Levine?

68
00:02:57,100 --> 00:02:59,000
One o'clock.

69
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,900
Remind me to leave at twelve forty-five.

70
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:04,400
Did you remember your other appointment for today?

71
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:05,900
No,

72
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:07,000
but I have a feeling

73
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:21,251
it's going to be too late when I do remember.

74
00:03:21,251 --> 00:03:22,700
Yes, Sam?

75
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:25,800
I just solved the mystery.

76
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:26,902
What did I forget?

77
00:03:26,902 --> 00:03:29,900
You have some guests in the reception room.

78
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:31,700
What? Who?

79
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:34,100
Mr. Harry Bennett and his daughter.

80
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:36,000
I remember!

81
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:37,600
Oh ... Harry!

82
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:41,700
I made a lunch date with him and his daughter weeks ago.

83
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:45,300
Is he a client?

84
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:47,600
He's a friend.

85
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:51,100
Well, he's here with his daughter to have lunch.

86
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:52,562
I met her at Thanksgiving,

87
00:03:52,562 --> 00:03:56,493
and I promised to have lunch with both of them today.

88
00:03:56,493 --> 00:03:58,100
Yes, indeed.

89
00:03:58,100 --> 00:03:59,800
What are you going to do about your appointment

90
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:03,900
with Mr. Levine?

91
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:07,954
Any suggestions?

92
00:04:07,954 --> 00:04:10,954
Oh!
